WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.001 --> 00:00:06.251
My name is Delia, and I am a 37-year-old weaver from Chinchero, Peru.

2
00:00:06.252 --> 00:00:13.270
My village is high in the Andes, located over one thousand feet above the Urubamba River.

3
00:00:13.271 --> 00:00:23.715
The view is breathtaking, but what makes Chinchero unique is that here, even though it is now 2008, we are preserving ancient traditions.

4
00:00:23.716 --> 00:00:32.083
In fact, Chinchero is just outside the city of Cuzco, which was once the center of the mighty Inca Empire.

5
00:00:32.084 --> 00:00:34.447
As a traditional master weaver,

6
00:00:34.448 --> 00:00:43.205
I have the privilege and responsibility of teaching my skills to the younger generation, uniting the past with the future.

7
00:00:43.206 --> 00:00:49.178
In fact, I’m waiting for Carla, a neighbor’s daughter, to come and take her weaving lesson.

8
00:00:49.179 --> 00:00:54.653
For many women like me, weaving is not just a craft but a way of life.

9
00:00:54.654 --> 00:01:03.471
Peruvian textiles have a rich history that can be traced as far back as 2500 BC, long before the Incas.

10
00:01:03.472 --> 00:01:11.058
By the time of the Inca Empire, in the 1500s, hand-weaving was widely practiced, even by men.

11
00:01:11.059 --> 00:01:22.975
The patterns woven into our textiles have always told stories, so our history was not just expressed orally but was also woven into cloth.

12
00:01:22.976 --> 00:01:33.198
More recently our weaving tradition was sadly being lost as many village people moved to cities, seeking money and a more modern way of life.

13
00:01:33.199 --> 00:01:38.966
But some, like me, have stayed in the village to keep our traditional culture alive.

14
00:01:38.967 --> 00:01:48.425
In 1996 a weaving center was established here, where women, young and old, can come to learn our unique weaving skills.

15
00:01:48.426 --> 00:01:55.269
It is called the Center for Traditional Textiles, and I started to work and teach here in

16
00:01:55.270 --> 00:02:03.447
2000. The mission of the center is to celebrate the history and traditions of Peruvian textiles and to educate others about

17
00:02:03.448 --> 00:02:10.413
them. After the women weavers gain expertise, their work is sold at the center or at local

18
00:02:10.414 --> 00:02:16.850
markets. This allows the women to gain financial independence or contribute to their family’s

19
00:02:16.851 --> 00:02:24.822
income. Practice is a must for good weavers, for we do not rely on fancy, high-speed machines to do the work for

20
00:02:24.823 --> 00:02:35.304
us. Our tools are our fingers—mostly our thumbs—and the yarn we weave comes from either alpaca or vicuna, animals that resemble small

21
00:02:35.305 --> 00:02:44.678
llamas. The alpaca are especially important to our way of life because in addition to their wool, they also supply meat, fertilizer, fuel, and

22
00:02:44.679 --> 00:02:49.088
leather. When I was a child, I often tended our sheep and

23
00:02:49.089 --> 00:02:53.376
alpaca. As I watched them graze, I practiced weaving jakima

24
00:02:53.377 --> 00:02:59.326
strips, small pieces that all the children in my village make to practice different textile

25
00:02:59.327 --> 00:03:07.774
patterns. This practice prepared me to learn more complicated patterns when I was seventeen and began my lessons with an older

26
00:03:07.775 --> 00:03:13.127
weaver. Alpaca and vicuna wool can be many shades of brown, cream, and

27
00:03:13.128 --> 00:03:20.884
gray, but we also dye the wool using natural sources, such as cochineal beetles, which live on prickly pear

28
00:03:20.885 --> 00:03:27.030
cactus. These beetles, when dried and crushed, yield a dye that makes our yarn a brilliant color of

29
00:03:27.031 --> 00:03:31.044
red. We also use eucalyptus leaves and beans to make

30
00:03:31.045 --> 00:03:36.731
dyes, and the vibrant colors from these plants range from pink to different shades of brown and

31
00:03:36.732 --> 00:03:44.004
green. How bright or deep the color is depends on the color of the plant and how long you leave the wool in the dye

32
00:03:44.005 --> 00:03:49.847
bath. Our weavers use a backstrap loom, which is very easy to use and carry

33
00:03:49.848 --> 00:03:53.963
around. Whenever I start a new textile, I make a new

34
00:03:53.964 --> 00:04:01.263
loom! First, I measure out the yarn for the warp, which consists of the threads that will run lengthwise in my

35
00:04:01.264 --> 00:04:07.499
textile. Then, I tie the warp to something immobile, such as a tree or a stake in the

36
00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:15.819
ground. For smaller textile pieces, some women use their own bodies by stretching out a leg and tying the warp to their

37
00:04:15.820 --> 00:04:20.427
foot. The other end of the warp has a strap that is tied around the weaver’s

38
00:04:20.428 --> 00:04:26.508
waist. Then, I interlace threads of yarn horizontally across the warp to create the

39
00:04:26.509 --> 00:04:29.727
pattern. The horizontal threads are called the

40
00:04:29.728 --> 00:04:35.180
woof. With all the different colors of yarn, we can make lots of patterns in our

41
00:04:35.181 --> 00:04:40.234
textiles. For example, there is a very special Incan pattern called the

42
00:04:40.235 --> 00:04:49.397
eye, which can be combined with other patterns to represent many things, from the burning of the sun to a gentle whirlpool in a meandering

43
00:04:49.398 --> 00:04:58.438
river. Each textile tells a story about our mountains, rivers, animals, and villages—sometimes even about the weaver

44
00:04:58.439 --> 00:05:04.374
herself. Because patterns and colors are chosen by the weaver to make each textile

45
00:05:04.375 --> 00:05:12.200
distinctive, dark colors may mean the weaver is sad, and bright colors may reflect the happiness she felt as she

46
00:05:12.201 --> 00:05:17.984
was weaving.The most important family textile is the manta, a cape that has many

47
00:05:17.985 --> 00:05:23.228
functions—from hauling fruit and firewood to carrying babies on mothers’ backs.

48
00:05:23.229 --> 00:05:26.507
Each village has its own unique manta pattern,

49
00:05:26.508 --> 00:05:32.658
so when others see you, they recognize what village you come from by the colorful design of your manta.

50
00:05:32.659 --> 00:05:40.241
When I weave outside, I sit on a manta, and whatever I am weaving, whether it is a belt, a hat, or a skirt,

51
00:05:40.242 --> 00:05:43.455
I put a lot of time and effort into each piece.

52
00:05:43.456 --> 00:05:51.724
Market day is special for the women of Chinchero, and we like to dress in our traditional handmade clothes when we go to the market.

53
00:05:51.725 --> 00:05:58.034
We wear colorful jackets, braid our hair, and wear our hats, called monteras, proudly.

54
00:05:58.035 --> 00:06:02.594
In our village everyone wears a montera with a round top.

55
00:06:02.595 --> 00:06:09.508
The brim of the hat curves toward its center, and inside the brim is a secret fold where we can store our money.

56
00:06:09.509 --> 00:06:16.813
When a tourist stops by and purchases one of my textiles, I just pop the money into my hat!

57
00:06:16.814 --> 00:06:23.615
The weaving center helps with the sale of our work, but it is up to each weaver to negotiate a price.

58
00:06:23.616 --> 00:06:29.382
To be honest, I don’t make a lot of money, only about three hundred dollars a year.

59
00:06:29.383 --> 00:06:34.090
But I am satisfied when I see how my weaving has made someone happy.

60
00:06:34.091 --> 00:06:47.082
Oh, here is Carla! Someday she will make our village proud and weave beautiful textiles that will tell the story of her generation.

